          Pick polyjacking (polyurethane foam) if the slab is a driveway you need back in use quickly, sits on soft or washed-out soil, or is decorative concrete where small holes matter, because foam is light, cures in roughly 15 to 30 minutes and resists erosion. Pick mudjacking if budget is the deciding factor, the soil is stable, the cause of settling has been fixed and you can stay off the slab for a day or more, because it typically costs less per square foot. Both methods raise a sunken slab by pumping material into the void beneath it, and both fail if the water that created the void keeps running. The details below explain where each one wins and where the marketing on both sides oversells.
        </p>

        <div id="side-by-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Do Mudjacking and Polyjacking Compare?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mudjacking and polyjacking solve the same problem. A concrete slab has sunk because the soil under it compacted, washed out or shifted, leaving a void. Both methods drill holes through the slab, pump material into the void under pressure, and use that pressure to lift the slab back toward level. Once the slab is up, the holes are patched and the material stays in place as the new support. The difference is the material, and almost every practical trade-off follows from it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Mudjacking</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Polyjacking (foam)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Material</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cement-based slurry (soil or sand, cement, water)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Expanding two-part polyurethane foam</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical hole size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.5 to 2 inches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 5/8 inch</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Material weight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 100+ lb per cubic foot</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Typically a few lb per cubic foot</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ready for cars</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly 24 hours or more</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly 15 to 30 minutes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Can erode if water runs under slab</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Does not absorb water; resists washout</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical cost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $3-6 per sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $5-25 per sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical longevity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Several years to a decade-plus with drainage fixed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often longer with drainage fixed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Best for</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget jobs on stable soil</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Daily-use slabs, weak soil, decorative concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The figures in the table are typical ranges, not specifications. Mix designs for mudjacking slurry vary from contractor to contractor, and lifting foams come in several densities for different jobs. Prices vary by region and change over time. But the direction of each comparison is consistent: slurry is heavier, cheaper and slower; foam is lighter, more expensive per square foot and faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            One point the table does not capture is skill. Either method done well beats the other done badly. A careful mudjacking crew that fixes the drainage problem will deliver a better result than a foam crew that over-lifts and cracks the slab. When you compare quotes, compare the companies as well as the methods.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are mostly trying to work out a budget, our <a href="/concrete-leveling-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete leveling cost</a> guide breaks down typical totals by job size, from a single sidewalk square to a whole driveway, and lists the extras that often get left off quotes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Does Each Method Actually Lift the Slab?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mudjacking, also called slabjacking or pressure grouting, is the older method. The contractor drills a pattern of holes through the slab, then pumps a slurry under it with a hydraulic pump. The slurry fills the void first, then, as pressure builds, starts pushing the slab up. The crew moves from hole to hole to raise the slab evenly, watching for the point where it meets its neighbors. Once the slab is at height, the holes are filled with a cement patch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Polyjacking uses a two-part polyurethane that is mixed at the injection gun and expands as it reacts. The contractor drills small holes, installs injection ports, and shoots the liquid under the slab. The foam expands to fill the void, then expands further and lifts the slab. Because the reaction is fast, the crew injects in small doses and watches the slab closely. Once the slab is at height, the ports come out and the holes are patched.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both methods depend on judgment. Lift too fast and the slab can crack or rise unevenly. Lift one side too far and you create a new trip hazard in the other direction. Experienced crews often use a straightedge or laser to track the slab as it moves, and they lift slowly near the target height. This is why a confident estimate of how a job will go, before any holes are drilled, is worth less than a crew's track record.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither method fixes the reason the void formed. Water from a downspout, a leaking line or poor grading carries soil away. Expansive clay soils shrink and swell with moisture. Fill that was not compacted properly when the house was built settles over the years. Animals burrow under patios. Lifting the slab restores its height, but if the cause is still active, the void will start forming again, under slurry or foam.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the first question a good contractor asks is what caused the settling. If they do not ask, ask them yourself, and make fixing the cause part of the job, whether that is extending a downspout, sealing joints or regrading soil away from the slab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="holes-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Do Hole Size and Material Weight Really Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hole size matters most on concrete people look at. Mudjacking holes, commonly around 1.5 to 2 inches across, are patched with cement and remain visible as a series of round dots, especially on colored, stamped or exposed aggregate concrete. Foam holes, commonly around 5/8 of an inch, are much less noticeable. On a plain gray driveway or a garage apron, many owners stop noticing either after a season. On a stamped patio, the difference is real.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fewer and smaller holes also mean less drilling stress on the slab. A slab that is already cracked or thin can be weakened a little more by each large hole. That does not make mudjacking unsafe on cracked concrete, but it is one reason foam is often preferred for older or more fragile slabs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight is the bigger technical difference. Cement slurry is heavy, typically weighing on the order of 100 pounds or more per cubic foot. Lifting foams commonly weigh just a few pounds per cubic foot. If the void is deep and the soil below is soft, pumping in a large volume of heavy slurry adds significant load to soil that has already proven it cannot hold the slab. That extra load can contribute to the slab settling again.
          </p>

          <p>
            Foam adds very little weight, which makes it the better match for soft, wet or poorly compacted soils, and for deep voids where a lot of material is needed. Some contractors also use foam to stabilize soils beneath a slab, injecting deeper to fill voids before lifting. Mudjacking remains a sound choice where the soil is firm and the void is modest, because the slurry provides solid support on a stable base.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Soft soil or deep void: lean foam</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the ground under the slab is soft, washed out or deeply undermined, foam's light weight is worth paying for. On firm soil with a shallow void, mudjacking's heavier slurry is not a problem.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Soon Can You Drive on It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the difference homeowners feel most. Polyurethane lifting foam reacts and reaches working strength quickly. Many contractors let you walk on the slab almost immediately and drive on it within roughly 15 to 30 minutes after the last injection. For a driveway that is your only way in and out, that means the job is done in an afternoon without moving the cars to the street overnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mudjacking slurry needs time to set before it can carry a vehicle. Contractors commonly ask you to stay off the slab with vehicles for at least 24 hours, and longer in cool or damp weather when cement-based materials set more slowly. Foot traffic is often fine sooner, but follow the crew's specific instructions, because driving on soft slurry can push the slab back down or crack it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weather also affects scheduling. Cement-based materials are sensitive to cold, and most contractors avoid mudjacking when freezing temperatures are expected. Foam can typically be installed across a wider range of temperatures, though manufacturers set limits for both the material and the slab surface. In cold climates, foam crews sometimes work later into the season than mudjacking crews.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a back patio or a side walkway, cure time rarely matters. You can avoid a patio for a day without inconvenience. For a commercial entrance, a busy driveway or a garage floor that has to hold a car tonight, foam's speed is a genuine advantage that can justify the price on its own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="longevity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which One Lasts Longer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Foam generally has the edge, with one important condition. Polyurethane lifting foam is closed-cell and does not absorb water, and it does not wash away the way soil-based slurry can if water keeps flowing under the slab. Mudjacking slurry is durable when it stays dry and supported, but where water keeps running beneath the slab, it can erode over time and leave a new void.
          </p>

          <p>
            The condition is that both methods depend on the cause of settling being fixed. A foam-lifted slab beside a downspout that pours water at its edge can still settle, because the soil beneath the foam keeps washing out. A mudjacked slab with good drainage, sealed joints and stable soil can last many years. The drainage fix is worth more than the choice of material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Typical expectations are a few years to a decade or more for mudjacking with drainage corrected, and often longer for foam. You will see much bigger claims in marketing on both sides. Be skeptical of any lifetime promise and read the warranty terms instead: what is covered, for how long, and what conditions void it.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more longevity factor is weight on soft soil, covered above. Heavy slurry over weak soil can contribute to the slab sinking again. That is not a flaw in mudjacking so much as a reason to choose foam in that specific situation. On firm soil, the weight difference has little effect on how long the repair lasts.
          </p>

          <p>
            After either lift, seal the joints between slabs and fill any cracks to keep water out. A flexible joint sealant over backer rod handles the joints, and our picks for the <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete crack filler</a> cover cracks from hairline to wide, moving to static. That small job is the cheapest insurance you can buy for a leveling repair.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Polyjacking Worth the Extra Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mudjacking typically costs around $3 to $6 per square foot, while polyjacking commonly runs around $5 to $25 per square foot. Foam is wider because it is often priced by the pound of material injected, and void size varies enormously. For many small jobs, both methods land near the contractor's minimum charge, so the real difference may be smaller than the per-square-foot numbers suggest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over the life of the repair, the gap can shrink further. If foam lasts meaningfully longer on your soil, and you avoid paying for a second lift, the higher upfront price can be the cheaper choice over a decade. If your soil is firm and the drainage is fixed, mudjacking may last long enough that foam's premium buys little.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are also costs neither quote shows. Mudjacking's larger holes may need color-matched patching on decorative concrete, which some contractors charge for. A day without the driveway may mean parking on the street or rearranging plans. Foam's speed and smaller holes reduce both. Weigh those against the headline price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare either method against replacement. Leveling, by either method, commonly costs a fraction of tearing out and repouring a slab, which is often $8 to $18 per square foot including demolition. If the concrete is sound, leveling is usually the better buy. If it is broken up, crumbling or near the end of its life, put the money toward a new slab instead. Our <a href="/concrete-driveway-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete driveway cost</a> guide covers replacement pricing in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. So Which Should You Pick?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose polyjacking when you need the slab back in service quickly, when the soil under it is soft, wet or washed out, when the void is deep, or when the concrete is decorative and you want small, discreet holes. It is also a reasonable choice for older or thinner slabs where fewer, smaller holes reduce drilling stress. For most daily-use driveways and garage aprons, foam is our default recommendation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose mudjacking when the budget is tight, the soil is firm, the void is shallow, the drainage problem has been fixed, and you can stay off the slab for a day or more. Sidewalks, patios and walkways on stable ground are classic mudjacking jobs, and an experienced mudjacking crew will deliver a sound repair at a lower price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose neither when the slab is shattered into many pieces, heavily spalled, or near the end of its service life. Lifting will not fix any of those problems. Get a replacement quote, or, if the slab is sound but the surface is worn, consider leveling followed by a resurfacer such as those in our <a href="/best-concrete-resurfacer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete resurfacer</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever method you choose, ask the contractor three things: what caused the settling and how it will be addressed, how many holes they will drill and how they will patch them, and what the warranty covers in writing. The answers tell you more about whether the repair will last than the choice between slurry and foam.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on price per square foot alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small jobs often hit the contractor minimum either way, so the gap between mudjacking and foam can be smaller than the headline rates suggest. Compare total price, hole patching, cure time, warranty and soil conditions. On soft soil, the cheaper method may simply mean paying for a second lift later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the drainage fix</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water washes out the soil under slabs. If a downspout still dumps beside the concrete or the yard slopes toward it, both slurry and foam can settle again. Extending downspouts and correcting grading costs little compared with the lift, and it is the single biggest factor in how long the repair lasts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mudjacking deep voids over soft soil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Slurry is heavy. Pumping a large volume of it into a deep cavity over soft or wet soil adds load to ground that has already failed to support the slab. In that situation, lightweight foam is usually the better match, even at a higher price, because it adds very little weight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving on slurry too early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mudjacking slurry needs time to set before it can carry a car. Driving on the slab before the contractor says it is ready can push it back down or crack it. Plan for at least a day off the slab, longer in cold weather, or choose foam if that is not possible.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Lifting concrete that should be replaced</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Neither method repairs a slab broken into many pieces or crumbling at the surface. Paying to lift a failing driveway buys a level surface that still looks bad and keeps deteriorating. If the concrete is worn out, get a replacement price and spend the leveling budget toward that instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is polyjacking better than mudjacking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most daily-use driveways and slabs over soft soil, yes. Foam is lighter, uses smaller holes, lets you drive on the slab in roughly 15 to 30 minutes and resists washout. Mudjacking is cheaper and works well on firm soil with modest voids. Contractor skill and fixing the cause of settling matter as much as the method you choose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can mudjacking be done over a previous foam repair, or the other way around?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often yes, but it depends on the conditions. Contractors can usually lift a previously mudjacked slab with foam, and in some cases mudjack a slab that was foamed before. The bigger question is why it sank again. If the original cause was not fixed, the second repair is likely to fail the same way, whatever material is used.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will polyurethane foam harm the soil or groundwater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Lifting foams are formulated to be inert once cured, and reputable manufacturers publish safety data and installation guidance for their products. The main concerns are during installation, when the liquid chemicals require proper handling by trained crews. If you have a well or are near sensitive water features, ask the contractor for the product's documentation before work starts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does mudjacking leave visible holes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Mudjacking holes, commonly around 1.5 to 2 inches across, are filled with a cement patch that usually shows as round dots. On plain gray concrete they fade into the background over time. On colored, stamped or exposed aggregate concrete, they are more noticeable. Foam holes are much smaller, around 5/8 inch, and easier to hide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can either method fix a cracked slab?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither method repairs cracks. Lifting can close a gap where one piece dropped and can make cracks less of a trip hazard, but the cracks themselves remain and occasionally widen or a new one forms during the lift. Plan to fill cracks and seal joints after leveling to keep water out and protect the repair.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does each method take to install?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical residential job takes a few hours with either method, often less than a day. Foam jobs can finish slightly faster because there is less material to mix and pump, and the slab is ready for traffic soon after. Mudjacking crews may take similar time on site, but the slab needs at least a day before vehicles use it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mudjack or polyjack in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mudjacking is usually avoided when freezing temperatures are expected, because cement-based slurry can be damaged if it freezes before setting. Foam can typically be installed across a wider range of temperatures, within the manufacturer's limits for the material and slab. Many foam contractors in cold climates keep working later into the fall than mudjacking crews.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

