        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Polyjacking is lighter, faster to use, less visible and usually longer lasting, which makes it our default for daily-use driveways, soft soils and decorative concrete. Mudjacking is cheaper and still a sound repair on firm soil with modest voids, as long as you can stay off the slab for a day.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Neither method fixes the water that caused the settling, and neither fixes a slab that is broken up or worn out. Fix the drainage, seal joints and cracks after the lift, and get a replacement price if the concrete is near the end of its life. Do those things and either method can give you years of level concrete.
          </p>
